<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Western Qin</h1><p class="st-meta">385 CE – 431 CE · capital Yuanchuan, Fuhan</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Qifu Xianbei kingdom in Gansu that rose from the Former Qin collapse and survived among stronger neighbours for nearly half a century.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qifu-clan">Qifu</a> were a <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/xianbei">Xianbei</a> clan settled around the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-river">Yellow River</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gansu">Gansu</a>. After <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/former-qin">Former Qin</a>'s collapse, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qifu-guoren">Qifu Guoren</a> declared himself ruler in 385, with his capital at Yuanchuan near modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhongyong-of-wu">Yuzhong</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/qin-xue-ju">Western Qin</a> was a small state of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/xianbei">Xianbei</a> herders and Chinese farmers, governed by tribal chiefs alongside Chinese officials. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qifu-gangui">Qifu Gangui</a> briefly submitted to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/later-qin">Later Qin</a> from 400 to 409 before restoring the state, which then conquered <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/southern-liang">Southern Liang</a> in 414. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> flourished: an inscription of 420 in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bingling-temple">Bingling Temple</a> caves near the later capital Fuhan is the oldest dated one in a Chinese cave temple.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Under attack from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/northern-liang">Northern Liang</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/helian-xia">Helian Xia</a>, the last ruler, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qifu-mumo">Qifu Mumo</a>, tried to flee to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/northern-wei">Northern Wei</a> but was captured by Helian Xia, which killed him and his clan in 431.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/western-qin.jpg" alt="Western Qin" width="320" height="227"><figcaption>Image: SY · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sixteen_Kingdoms_391_AD_(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>385 CE – 431 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Yuanchuan, Fuhan</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Qifu Guoren, Qifu Gangui, Qifu Chipan</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Xianbei, Middle Chinese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Qin (Qifu clan)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=408&amp;focus=western-qin" aria-label="Western Qin on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7296 876 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Western Qin lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7616 1132L7769 1191M7616 1132L7713 1068"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7769 1191h0M7713 1068h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7616 1132h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7616 1132h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=408&amp;focus=western-qin">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
